--TEST--
Core\Db::quoteIdentifier bounds an identifier's first character on both sides — `_` leads and a digit does not — and its refusal names the name — ADR 0024 § 4
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The rule is a letter or `_`, then letters, digits or `_`. This case names the
// accepted first characters and the refused one together: a member that let a
// leading digit through would still look right on every line of the sibling
// case, which only asks about names it accepts.

// Accepted: `_` leads on its own, and a digit is legal everywhere after one.
echo Core\Db::quoteIdentifier("_"), "\n";
echo Core\Db::quoteIdentifier("_9"), "\n";
echo Core\Db::quoteIdentifier("a9"), "\n";

// Refused: the same digit in the first position. The message names the name,
// which is what tells a caller which of its identifiers was the bad one.
try {
    echo Core\Db::quoteIdentifier("9a"), "\n";
} catch (LogicError $leading) {
    echo $leading->message, "\n";
}

// The empty name is the other end of the same bound: there is no identifier
// with no characters, so an empty string is a name the caller assembled and
// got wrong rather than one to pass through.
try {
    echo Core\Db::quoteIdentifier(""), "\n";
} catch (LogicError $empty) {
    echo $empty->message, "\n";
}
--EXPECT--
_
_9
a9
Core\Db::quoteIdentifier() takes a bare identifier — a letter or `_`, then letters, digits or `_` — and `9a` is not one. `Core\Db` has no connection and so no dialect to delimit for, so a name that needs delimiting is refused rather than quoted for the wrong backend
Core\Db::quoteIdentifier() takes a bare identifier — a letter or `_`, then letters, digits or `_` — and `` is not one. `Core\Db` has no connection and so no dialect to delimit for, so a name that needs delimiting is refused rather than quoted for the wrong backend
